"SPRInG: Continual LLM Personalization via Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1)\nciteturn30950view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09974v1\",\"lineno\":123}); Total lines: 703\nL96: The objective is then to update the user adapter from $\\phi_{u}^{(t-1)}$ to $\\phi_{u}^{(t)}$ to reflect the evolving user preferences encoded in $\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}$, while maintaining a non-parametric retrieval buffer $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}$ to mitigate forgetting important past information. We denote the user-adapted model at period $t$ as $\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t)}$, which is the frozen base model $\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta}$ equipped with the current user adapter $\\phi_{u}^{(t)}$.\nL97: With the user-adapted model, the generation of response $a$ for a given query $q$ is modeled by conditional probability $P(a|q;\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t)})$.\nL98: Standard CL approaches generally update the adapter $\\phi_{u}^{(t)}$ by minimizing the negative log-likelihood over the entire incoming stream $\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}$. However, such an indiscriminate update strategy is inappropriate for capturing subtle preference drift. Since user interactions are often noisy or context-dependent, treating all samples equally could obscure meaningful signals and cause the model to overfit to temporary contexts rather than genuine preference shifts.\nL99: To address this, we propose a method that selectively utilizes interactions indicative of true preference drift, thereby achieving effective personalization while filtering out noise.\nL100: ## 3 Selective Parametric Adaptation and Retrieval-Interpolated Generation\nL101: In this section, we propose SPRInG (S elective P arametric adaptation and R etrieval-In terpolated G eneration), which conducts a two-phase process for continual LLM personalization. First, drift-driven selective adaptation (Sec. cite9†3.1 ) selectively updates the adapter by prioritizing interactions with high drift scores, capturing genuine preference shifts while retaining critical interactions in a replay buffer. Second, selective retrieval-interpolated generation (Sec.\nL102: cite13†3.2 ) dynamically fuses this parametric knowledge with historical evidence during inference. This separation ensures that the adapter focuses on learning persistent preference patterns, leaving complex nuances to be handled by the retrieval memory. See Figure cite73†2 for an overview.\nL103: ### 3.1 Training: Drift-driven selective adaptation and residual replay\nL104: The training phase of SPRInG is designed to efficiently capture user preferences from the incoming interaction stream through a three-step pipeline. First, we perform drift selection by comparing likelihoods between the base LLM and the same model equipped with the current adapter to isolate high-novelty interactions. Next, we execute a parametric update, fine-tuning the user adapter on these identified samples to internalize genuine preference shifts.\nL105: Finally, we construct a residual replay buffer by re-evaluating the samples post-update; interactions that remain challenging are stored in the buffer to mitigate forgetting for the inference phase. The overall procedure is summarized in Algorithm cite74†1 .\nL106: #### Drift sample selection.\nL107: To identify interactions to use for an adapter update, we define a scoring function that quantifies sample novelty with respect to the current adapter, thereby distinguishing genuine preference drift from uninformative noise. Let $p_{\\texttt{base}}=P(a\\mid q;\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta})$ denote the likelihood of the response $a$ under the base model, and $p_{\\texttt{adap}}^{(t-1)}=P(a\\mid q;\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t-1)})$ denote the likelihood under model adapted with the current adapter.\nL108: Then, we define drift score $\\mathcal{S}(q,a)$ as follows:\nL109:  | $$\\mathcal{S}(q,a)=(\\underbrace{-\\log\\frac{p_{\\texttt{adap}}^{(t-1)}}{p_{\\texttt{base}}}}_{\\text{Novelty Term}})+\\underbrace{\\alpha\\log p_{\\texttt{base}}}_{\\text{Quality Term}}$$  |  | (1)\nL110: \nL111: where $\\alpha$ is a balancing hyperparameter. We prioritize samples that maximize this score, balancing two critical objectives.\nL112: \nL113:   * $\\circ$\nL114: The Novelty Term assigns higher priority to samples with unfamiliar preference patterns. Thus, if the adapter already outperforms the base model ($p_{\\texttt{adap}}\\gg p_{\\texttt{base}}$), the novelty score decreases as the preference is considered already internalized.\nL115: \nL116:   * $\\circ$\nL117: The Quality Term filters for linguistic validity, using a high $p_{\\texttt{base}}$ to identify well-formed and intelligible samples. This term acts as a precautionary safeguard against pseudo-novelty, preventing noisy outliers or gibberish from being erroneously selected simply because they are difficult for the adapter to predict.\nL118: \nL119: Finally, we construct the drift set $\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}$ by retaining the top $p\\%$ of the samples:\nL120:  | $$\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}=\\{(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}\\mid\\mathcal{S}(q,a)\\geq\\tau_{p}\\}$$  |  | (2)\nL121: \nL122: where $\\tau_{p}$ is a score threshold at the $p$-th percentile of the current batch $\\mathcal{D}_{u}^{(t)}$.\nL123: #### Parametric adapter update.\nL124: We employ Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) (cite75†Hu et al., 2022 ) to efficiently evolve the user model, which has been demonstrated in the context of LLM personalization (cite65†Tan et al., 2024 ). For the initial period ($t=0$), we utilize a pre-trained adapter $\\phi_{u}^{(0)}$ established on the complete interaction history. For subsequent periods ($t>0$), we initialize the current adapter with parameters from previous step ($\\phi_{u}^{(t)}\\leftarrow\\phi_{u}^{(t-1)}$) to inherit prior user preferences.\nL125: We then fine-tune $\\phi_{u}^{(t)}$ exclusively on the selected drift set $\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}$. By restricting training to high-novelty samples, we force the adapter to focus solely on acquiring new preference patterns. This targeted approach not only minimizes computational overhead but also prevents the model from overfitting to well-internalized stationary data.\nL126: #### Residual replay buffer construction.\nL127: \nL128: Since parametric updates favor generalizable patterns, weight-encoded knowledge often misses sparse but critical historical nuances. To mitigate this, we maintain a non-parametric replay buffer $\\mathcal{B}_{u}$ as a complementary memory, preserving interactions that remain challenging for the updated adapter.\nL129: \nL130: At period $t$, we first merge the newly identified drift samples with the existing buffer to form a candidate pool:\nL131:  | $$\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}=\\mathcal{D}_{\\texttt{drift}}^{(t)}\\cup\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t-1)}.$$  |  | (3)\nL132: We then re-evaluate all samples in $\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}$ using the updated adapter $\\phi_{u}^{(t)}$; we compute the post-update score $\\mathcal{S}^{\\prime}_{t}(q,a):=\\mathcal{S}(q,a;\\mathcal{M}_{\\theta},\\phi_{u}^{(t)})$, which quantifies the residual difficulty after the parametric update.\nL133: This step is crucial because many drift samples that initially appear novel may become well-internalized after the adapter is updated; re-scoring allows to filter such redundant data and find samples that remain poorly predicted.\nL134: Finally, we update the replay buffer $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}$ under a fixed budget $N_{\\max}$. When $|\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}|\\leq N_{\\max}$, we directly set $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}=\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}$ without any filtering. Otherwise, we employ a Global Highest retention policy to satisfy the budget constraint; we rank the candidates by their post-update scores $\\mathcal{S}_{t}^{\\prime}$ in descending order and retain only the top-$N_{\\max}$ samples to form $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}$ (Eq. cite76†4 ).\nL135: We provide an ablation study comparing this policy with alternative strategies in Appendix cite32†A.2 .\nL136:  | $$\\hskip-20.0pt\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}=\\begin{cases}\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}&\\text{if }|\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}|\\leq N_{\\max}\\\\\nL137: \\text{Top-}N_{\\max}\\{(q,a)\\in\\mathcal{C}_{u}^{(t)}\\}&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}$$  |  | (4)\nL138: ### 3.2 Inference: Gated replay-augmented generation and logit interpolation\nL139: In the inference stage, SPRInG employs a Token-level Probability Interpolation strategy, which dynamically balances the parametric knowledge of $\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t)}$ with an explicit non-parametric context from the replay buffer $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}$ at each decoding step.\nL140: This inference-time approach effectively addresses the limitation where parametric weights often fail to capture specific recent contexts or nuances that have not yet been fully internalized, whereas standard retrieval risks introducing irrelevant noise. The overall procedure is presented in Algorithm cite77†2 .\nL141: #### Relevance gating mechanism.\nL142: \nL143: Given a test query $q$, we first retrieve a candidate set $\\mathcal{R}_{q}$ containing the top-$k$ relevant interactions from $\\mathcal{B}_{u}^{(t)}$. To prevent the detrimental effects of irrelevant retrieved contexts, we introduce a relevance gating mechanism using a threshold $\\sigma$. Specifically, we define $\\mathbf{c}_{\\texttt{ret}}$ as the concatenation of gated interactions from $\\mathcal{R}_{q}$:\nL144:  | $$\\mathbf{c}_{\\texttt{ret}}=\\textsc{Concat}\\{h\\in\\mathcal{R}_{q}\\mid\\textsc{Sim}(q,h)>\\sigma\\}$$  |  | (5)\nL145: \nL146: where $\\textsc{Sim}(\\cdot)$ is a retrieval function. This mechanism ensures that the model is augmented only when strong historical evidence exists. If no documents pass, $\\mathbf{c}_{\\texttt{ret}}$ remains empty, reverting to purely parametric generation.\nL147: #### Dual-path logit interpolation.\nL148: \nL149: To dynamically balance internalized preferences with retrieved evidence during decoding steps, we employ a logit interpolation strategy between two different paths using the same user-adapted model $\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t)}$.\nL150: \nL151:   * $\\circ$\nL152: \nL153: Parametric Path takes only the query $q$ as input of $\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t)}$, relying solely on encoded knowledge in adapter’s weight to guide generation.\nL154: \nL155:   * $\\circ$\nL156: Retrieval-augmented Path inputs the query $q$ augmented with the retrieved context $\\mathbf{c}_{\\texttt{ret}}$, providing the model with explicit historical evidence.\nL157: \nL158: Specifically, at each decoding step $j$, we compute the next-token distributions given the prefix $a_{<j}$:\nL159:  | $\\displaystyle p_{\\texttt{par}}$  | $\\displaystyle=P(a_{j}\\mid q,a_{<j};\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t)})$  |  | (6)\nL160:  | $\\displaystyle p_{\\texttt{ret}}$  | $\\displaystyle=P(a_{j}\\mid q,\\mathbf{c}_{\\texttt{ret}},a_{<j};\\mathcal{M}_{u}^{(t)})$  |  | (7)\nL161: \nL162: We then fuse these distributions via linear interpolation with a mixing coefficient $\\lambda$:\nL163: \nL164:  | $$p_{\\texttt{fin}}=\\lambda\\cdot p_{\\texttt{ret}}+(1-\\lambda)\\cdot p_{\\texttt{par}}$$  |  | (8)\nL165: Finally, the selected token $\\hat{a}_{j}\\sim p_{\\text{fin}}$ is appended to the prefix $a_{<j}$ for both paths, ensuring synchronization for the next decoding step.\nL166: ## 4 Experiments\nL167: \nL168: ### 4.1 Setups\nL169: #### Datasets and metrics.\nL170: We evaluate the effectiveness of SPRInG on the LongLaMP (cite71†Kumar et al., 2024 ) benchmark, which is designed to assess long-form personalized text generation capabilities. Specifically, Abstract Generation and Review Writing are selected for their accessible temporal metadata, unlike Topic Writing (no timestamps) and Email Generation (restricted access). For the Abstract Generation task, we sort samples based on the publication year.\nL171: However, in the case of the Review Writing task, the LongLaMP dataset does not contain timestamps; hence, we retrieve the reviewTime by matching samples to the original Amazon Product Review dataset (cite78†Ni et al., 2019 ) and sort them accordingly. Based on this temporal data, we filter users based on their data volume to ensure sufficient history for longitudinal analysis, resulting in a final set of 100 users.\nL172: These users are further categorized into Small and Large groups to evaluate performance across different interaction scales. To simulate a realistic continual personalization scenario, we construct a temporal stream for each user by partitioning it into five sequential periods, denoted as Period 0–4. In this setup, Period 0 serves as the initial data for user profiling, while Periods 1–4 constitute the stream where new data is sequentially observed.\nL173: Within each period, we implement a split where the earliest 90% of samples are utilized for the training, and the most recent 10% are reserved as the test set. To evaluate performance, we employ ROUGE-1 and ROUGE-L to measure content fidelity and style alignment with the ground truth. We present the results for each individual period as well as the average performance (Period Avg) to show the overall trends.\nL174: Table 1: Main results. ROUGE-1 and ROUGE-L scores across periods (1–4) and their average for Abstract Generation and Review Writing tasks. All experiments are conducted using Gemma-3-4B-IT. Bold indicates the best performance, and underline indicates the second best.\nL175:  | Abstract Generation\nL176:  | Period 1  | Period 2  | Period 3  | Period 4  | Period Avg\nL177: Methods  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L  | ROUGE-1  | ROUGE-L\nL178: Base  | 0.302  | 0.147  | 0.302  | 0.148  | 0.300  | 0.148  | 0.298  | 0.144  | 0.301  | 0.147\nL179: RAG each  | 0.304  | 0.162  | 0.302  | 0.160  | 0.301  | 0.160  | 0.299  | 0.156  | 0.301  | 0.159\nL180: RAG accum  | 0.301  | 0.161  | 0.300  | 0.160  | 0.297  | 0.158  | 0.296  | 0.155  | 0.299  | 0.158\nL181: PAG each  | 0.304  | 0.144  | 0.303  | 0.145  | 0.299  | 0.144  | 0.304  | 0.144  | 0.302  | 0.144\nL182: PAG accum  | 0.306  | 0.145  | 0.303  | 0.144  | 0.303  | 0.145  | 0.307  | 0.146  | 0.305  | 0.145\nL183: OPPU each  | 0.321  | 0.155  | 0.320  | 0.155  | 0.316  | 0.153  | 0.318  | 0.150  | 0.319  | 0.153\nL184: OPPU accum  | 0.319  | 0.154  | 0.305  | 0.151  | 0.292  | 0.144  | 0.282  | 0.136  | 0.300  | 0.146\nL185: CAMA  | 0.336  | 0.162  | 0.338  | 0.166  | 0.337  | 0.166  | 0.337  | 0.162  | 0.337  | 0.164\nL186: SAR  | 0.333  | 0.163  | 0.334  | 0.164  | 0.324  | 0.160  | 0.314  | 0.151  | 0.326  | 0.159\nL187: O-LoRA  | 0.334  | 0.163  | 0.303  | 0.148  | 0.300  | 0.147  | 0.299  | 0.145  | 0.309  | 0.151\nL188: EWC-LoRA  | 0.337  | 0.163  | 0.335  | 0.165  | 0.328  | 0.163  | 0.323  | 0.157  | 0.331  | 0.162\nL189: SPRInG (Ours)  | 0.346  | 0.188  | 0.353  | 0.192  | 0.351  | 0.191  | 0.351  | 0.190  | 0.350  | 0.190\nL190:  | Review Writing\nL191:  | Period 1  | Period 2  | Period 3  | Period 4  | Period Avg\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImproving Chain-of-Thought for Logical Reasoningvia Attention-Aware Intervention (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09805v1)\nciteturn30950view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09805v1\",\"lineno\":117}); Total lines: 901\nL82: In Symbolic-Aided CoT prompting (cite53†Nguyen et al., 2025 ), the authors first tag the rules and facts in the logical reasoning problem as variables (e.g., Rule1). A set of symbolic tokens (e.g., =>, F) is then injected into the prompt to represent the structure of the reasoning flow. The inference process is constructed using reasoning operators, including rule selection, new premise inference, and knowledge-base (KB) updating.\nL83: The experiments are performed using three-shot Symbolic-Aided CoT prompting on the ProofWriter dataset (Table cite56†1 ), employing the Qwen3-8B LLM. We visualize the attention matrix values in Fig. cite57†2 . These values are computed as $\\mathrm{softmax}(qk^{\\top}/\\sqrt{d})$, where $q$ and $k$ denote the query and key matrices in the last transformer decoder layer, and $d$ is their dimensionality cite58†Vaswani et al. (2017) . The x- and y-axes correspond to the key and query tokens, respectively.\nL84: Table 1: Symbolic-Aided CoT prompting. The colored text span were used for attention visualization.\nL85: …\nL86: # (Rule8): Rough things are blue.\nL87: # (Rule9): Red, quiet things are blue.\nL88: # (Rule10): All rough, blue things are nice.\nL89: # (Rule11): If something is quiet and round then it is not nice.\nL90: # (Rule12): If Harry is red then Harry is rough.\nL91: # (Rule13): If something is nice then it is round.\nL92: # (Rule14): If something is round then it is red.\nL93: # (Rule15): Red things are not quiet.\nL94: # (Question): Based on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Gary is red.\nL95: # (Answer): Start from the object and their condition mentioned in the question to collect relevant facts: Gary, is red\nL96: # KB = {}\nL97: => Rule6 = “Gary is rough”\nL98: # KB = {Gary is rough}\nL99: => F(KB(“Gary is rough”), Rule8) => “Gary is blue”\nL100: # KB = {Gary is rough, Gary is blue}\nL101: => F(KB(“Gary is blue”), Rule10) => “Gary is nice”\nL102: # KB = {Gary is rough, Gary is blue, Gary is nice}\nL103: => F(KB(“Gary is nice”), Rule13) => “Gary is round”\nL104: # KB = {Gary is rough, Gary is blue, Gary is nice, Gary is round}\nL105: => F(KB(“Gary is round”), Rule14 ) => “Gary is red”\nL106: # KB = {Gary is rough, Gary is blue, Gary is nice, Gary is round, Gary is red}\nL107: # valid the question with current infered premies\nL108: => Validate(Gary is red, KB(“Gary is red”)) = True.\nL109: #### Observations.\nL110: \nL111: Attention structuring. Firstly, consistent with the observations of cite53†Nguyen et al. (2025) , we found that these logical symbols are represented separately in the hidden semantic space. In the self-attention representations, these symbols receive significantly less attention than informative words such as “Gary” or “round”. This discrepancy causes discontinuities in the self-attention matrix (shown as white or colorless points in Fig. cite57†2 ).\nL112: Attention pattern. Secondly, we found that a subset of attention heads in the self-attention layer reflects the reasoning operators, as shown by the highlighted patterns corresponding to strong word-pair dependencies. In the Transformer decoder’s self-attention layer, the information of a query token vector is aggregated from previous token vectors, with the proportion of contribution represented in the attention matrix.\nL113: In the first image of Fig. cite57†2 (head 28), each token primarily attends to itself, without incorporating information from other tokens. The highlighted points mainly correspond to informative words (e.g., “Gary”, “round”). We identify this type of head as an anchor head, which serves to retain premise-related information. In the second image of Fig. cite57†2 (head 8), the newly generated premise tends to distribute its information to subsequent inference steps.\nL114: For example, the premise “Gary is round” contributes to the representations of many following tokens, corresponding to the vertical highlights observed in the attention map. We identify this type of head as an aggregation head, which supports matching the generated premises with the applicable rules in the logical inference process. The last image (head 22) presents another attention pattern characterized by short diagonal alignments, reflecting the copying of an entire information span to another span.\nL115: For instance, the premise “Gary is round” is copied to the knowledge-base (KB) variable after it is generated. We identify this type of head as a copy head.\nL116: #### Motivation.\nL117: Building on the previous observations, we address the second research question (RQ2): how can we modify the attention matrix to effectively inject prior knowledge into LLMs? In the Symbolic-Aided CoT framework, each rule is tagged with a symbolic identifier (Rule<index>), requiring the model to retrieve and maintain the corresponding rule information throughout the reasoning process.\nL118: However, this design can lead to information decay in long reasoning chains, as the identifiers and their associated rule contents often appear in distant spans (e.g., Rule14 in Table cite56†1 ). Furthermore, in copy heads, the copying behavior typically occurs within the same or paraphrased token spans, whereas the original rule content should instead be propagated to the representation of its identifier during reasoning.\nL119: Similarly, in anchor heads, attention tends to focus on the identifiers themselves rather than capturing the semantics of the underlying rule content.\nL120: To address this issue, we propose a mechanism that first categorizes anchor heads and copy heads and then adaptively reweights them to reinforce the linkage between rule identifiers and their original rule representations. In essence, this process injects structural prior knowledge into the attention mechanism, ensuring that each rule reference remains grounded in its semantic content during reasoning.\nL121: ## 3 Methodology\nL122: \nL123: In this section, we present the details of our proposed method for analyzing attention head patterns to select attention head patterns (e.g., anchor heads), and our Attention-aware Intervention mechanism for reweighting their attention scores to inject prior knowledge into LLMs.\nL124: Transformer decoder-only architectures are the dominant design for recent large language models (LLMs) (cite59†Radford et al., 2019 ; cite37†Achiam et al., 2023 ; cite38†Dubey et al., 2024 ; cite39†Yang et al., 2025 ). In these models, semantic features representing the meaning of an input sequence are computed within the multi-head attention layers. Specifically, the self-attention mechanism captures dependencies among word pairs.\nL125: For generation tasks, a causal mask ($M=M^{\\mathrm{Causal}}$) is applied to prevent tokens from attending to future positions (cite58†Vaswani et al., 2017 ). Given, query and key matrices, $q,k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times d}$ general formulation of attention is:\nL126:  | $\\displaystyle S$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{qk^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d}}$  |  | (1)\nL127:  | $\\displaystyle A$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathrm{softmax}\\left(S+M\\right)$  |  | (2)\nL128:  | $\\displaystyle h^{\\mathrm{attn}}$  | $\\displaystyle=A\\cdot v$  |  | (3)\nL129: Beyond this standard use, masked matrices have also been explored as a means of injecting prior knowledge into Transformer architectures. For example, in neural machine translation, masks have been employed to encode syntactic dependency structures (cite60†Bugliarello and Okazaki, 2020 ; cite61†Wang et al., 2019 ) or to model localness (cite62†Yang et al., 2018 ).\nL130: Similarly, in emotion recognition tasks, masks have been utilized to represent intra- and inter-utterance relationships (cite63†Liu et al., 2022 ; cite64†Zhao et al., 2022 ). However, in the context of in-context learning with LLMs, no training is performed, which makes customizing masks for prior knowledge injection significantly more challenging.\nL131: Motivated by these observations, we propose an efficient method to inject reference information into the decoder-only Transformer architecture by reweighting the masked matrix $M$.\nL132: ### 3.1 Attention Head Analysis\nL133: \nL134: In this step, our objective is to identify attention patterns by analyzing the relative positional relationships between token pairs exhibiting strong dependencies. Given an attention score matrix $A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times L}$ (as defined in Eq. cite65†2 ), where $L$ denotes the length of the input sequence, each element $(A_{ij})$ represents the dependency score between the $i$-th query token and the $j$-th key token.\nL135:  | $\\displaystyle H_{ij}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\begin{cases}1&\\text{if $(A_{i,j}>\\mathrm{threshold})\\wedge(i\\geq j)$}\\\\\nL136: 0&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}$  |\nL137:  | $\\displaystyle s^{\\mathrm{diagonal}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\sum_{i,j\\mid i,j<L-1}(H_{i,j}\\wedge H_{i+1,j+1})}{\\sum_{i,j}H_{i,j}}$  |  | (4)\nL138:  | $\\displaystyle s^{\\mathrm{vertical}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\sum_{i,j\\mid j<L-1}(H_{i,j}\\wedge H_{i,j+1})}{\\sum_{i,j}H_{i,j}}$  |  | (5)\nL139:  | $\\displaystyle s^{\\mathrm{horizontal}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{\\sum_{i,j\\mid i<L-1}(H_{i,j}\\wedge H_{i+1,j})}{\\sum_{i,j}H_{i,j}}$  |  | (6)\nL140:  | $\\displaystyle\\textrm{pattern}(A)$  | $\\displaystyle=(s^{\\mathrm{diagonal}},s^{\\mathrm{vertical}},s^{\\mathrm{horizontal}})$  |  | (7)\nL141: To this end, the pattern of each attention head is characterized by three directional scores: $s^{\\mathrm{diagonal}}$, $s^{\\mathrm{vertical}}$, $s^{\\mathrm{horizontal}}$, which quantify the relative density of attention edges along the diagonal, vertical, and horizontal directions, respectively. Aggregation heads typically exhibit high vertical scores, reflecting strong dependencies between informative tokens.\nL142: For instance, in head 8 of Fig. cite57†2 , the token “round” receives concentrated attention from subsequent query tokens. In contrast, anchor heads generally display high diagonal but low vertical and horizontal scores, indicating self-focused attention patterns.\nL143: ### 3.2 Attention Head Reweighting\nL144: This component is designed to inject human prior knowledge into LLMs through a masked matrix ($M$). By default, this matrix is defined as the causal mask ($M^{\\mathrm{Causal}}$), which prevents information flow from future tokens during generation. However, in Symbolic-Aided CoT prompting for logical reasoning tasks, the rule identifier tokens (Rule<number>) should be semantically linked with their corresponding rule content.\nL145: To enable this, we construct two subsets of token pairs: $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{Ref}}$, which includes pairs connecting a rule identifier to tokens within its own rule span, and $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{NoRef}}$, which includes pairs connecting a rule identifier to tokens’ index in other rules. Intuitively, dependencies in $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{Ref}}$ should be encouraged, whereas those in $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{NoRef}}$ should be suppressed.\nL146: For instance, in Fig. cite44†1 , Rule21 illustrates such relationships within the $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{Ref}}$ set.\nL147: Given the scaled dot-product values $S$ computed by Eq. cite66†1 , and considering that the range of hidden-state values differs across attention heads, we adaptively compute median-based normalization for each head during the reweighting process. Accordingly, we define two masking matrices, $M^{\\mathrm{Ref}}_{i,j}$ and $M^{\\mathrm{NoRef}}_{i,j}$, to reinforce the representation of rule identifiers throughout the reasoning process.\nL148: These matrices can be applied independently or combined to integrate both effects, and are used to re-weight selected attention heads in the previous step.\nL149:  | $\\displaystyle M^{\\mathrm{Causal}}_{i,j}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\begin{cases}-\\infty&\\textrm{if}\\quad i<j\\\\\nL150: 0&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}$  |  | (8)\nL151:  | $\\displaystyle M^{\\mathrm{NoRef}}_{i,j}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\begin{cases}-\\infty&\\textrm{if}\\quad(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{NoRef}}\\\\\nL152: 0&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}$  |  | (9)\nL153:  | $\\displaystyle M^{\\mathrm{Ref}}_{i,j}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\begin{cases}c\\times\\textrm{median}(S)+b&\\textrm{if}\\,\\,(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{Ref}}\\\\\nL154: 0&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}$  |  | (10)\nL155:  | $\\displaystyle M^{\\mathrm{Final}}$  | $\\displaystyle=M^{\\mathrm{Ref}}+M^{\\mathrm{NoRef}}+M^{\\mathrm{Causal}}$  |  | (11)\nL156: Here, $c$ (reweighting coefficient) and $b$ (reweighting bias) are configuration parameters that control the degree of reweighting (Eq. cite67†10 ). Larger values of $c$ strengthen the connections within the $\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{Ref}}$ set in alignment with the original attention matrix values. In contrast, increasing the constant $b$ introduces a uniform bias that does not depend on the original attention values.\nL157: ## 4 Experiments and Result Analysis\nL158: \nL159: In this section, we present the experimental settings, datasets, and evaluation metrics used to assess our proposed method. We then report the main results compared with several strong baselines across multiple logical reasoning benchmarks. Finally, we provide an in-depth analysis and ablation studies to investigate the effectiveness of each component in our approach.\nL160: \nL161: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup\nL162: #### Datasets.\nL163: We evaluate our approach on four widely used benchmark datasets for logical reasoning and one mathematical reasoning dataset. (1) ProofWriter cite45†Tafjord et al. (2021) : we adopt the subset constructed under the open-world assumption, where each instance can take one of three possible labels–true, false, or unknown. Following the experimental setup of cite42†Pan et al. (2023) ; cite53†Nguyen et al.\nL164: (2025) , we focus on the subset with the highest reasoning complexity (five-hop reasoning), which comprises 600 examples. (2) ProntoQA cite68†Saparov and He (2023) : similar to ProofWriter, this dataset contains multi-hop logical reasoning problems. Consistent with prior studies cite69†Qi et al. (2025) ; cite53†Nguyen et al. (2025) , we use the most challenging subset that requires five-hop reasoning, containing 500 evaluation samples. (3) LogicalDeduction 【70†Srivastava et al.\n"
